Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

## Background and Motivation

`MSBuildWorkspace` loads projects through its BuildHost, where the underlying `Microsoft.Build.Evaluation.Project` and its resolved import closure are available during evaluation. Consumers that need reproducible evaluation context cannot obtain that exact closure through the current public workspace surface; re-evaluating the project later can observe different target-framework/global-property context, and the BinaryLogger `ProjectImports=ZipFile` path has proved incomplete for this hosted flow.

A narrow accessor would let consumers inspect the resolved imports from the same per-target-framework evaluation used to produce each loaded Roslyn project. The captured content identity should be computed from each `ProjectRootElement.RawXml` while the evaluation is alive. Include the root project separately because `Project.Imports` excludes it. Preserve duplicate import occurrences and nested imports; conditional imports that were not evaluated should be absent. Return SHA-256 fingerprints rather than entire XML payloads to keep BuildHost RPC responses bounded. This API would provide import content identity, not claim that all evaluation inputs are sufficient for cache reuse.

## Proposed API

```csharp
namespace Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.MSBuild
{
    public sealed class MSBuildWorkspace
    {
        public MSBuildProjectImportSnapshot? GetProjectImportSnapshot(ProjectId projectId);
    }

    public sealed class MSBuildProjectImportSnapshot
    {
        public string ProjectFilePath { get; }
        public string ProjectFileContentHash { get; }
        public ImmutableArray<MSBuildProjectImportInfo> Imports { get; }
    }

    public sealed class MSBuildProjectImportInfo
    {
        public string FilePath { get; }
        public string ContentHash { get; }
    }
}
```

The snapshot is associated with a loaded `ProjectId`, so each multi-target-framework Roslyn project receives the result from its matching evaluation. The method returns `null` when the host did not provide a snapshot (for example, an older or mixed-version BuildHost). Paths are absolute, hashes are SHA-256 over the evaluated `ProjectRootElement.RawXml`, and the imports array preserves resolved occurrence order, including repeated imports.

## Usage Examples

```csharp
using var workspace = MSBuildWorkspace.Create();
var solution = await workspace.OpenSolutionAsync(solutionPath);

foreach (var project in solution.Projects)
{
    var snapshot = workspace.GetProjectImportSnapshot(project.Id);
    if (snapshot is null)
        continue; // Do not treat unavailable provenance as a complete cache key.

    cacheKey.Add(snapshot.ProjectFilePath, snapshot.ProjectFileContentHash);
    foreach (var import in snapshot.Imports)
        cacheKey.Add(import.FilePath, import.ContentHash);
}
```

## Alternative Designs

- Re-evaluate the project in the caller: rejected because it may not use the same target framework, global properties, SDK state, or ProjectCollection context as the evaluation that loaded the Roslyn project.
- Use `MSBuildAllProjects` or the public BinaryLogger import ZIP: rejected as complete inventories for this path; generated controls showed relevant conditional/content-only changes were missed.
- Use reflection or replace Roslyn's private BuildHost launch path: unsupported and brittle.

## Risks

- The API adds a new public Workspaces contract and should go through the documented Roslyn API review before implementation.
- BuildHost/client version skew must be handled without requiring new DTO fields from older hosts; unavailable snapshots should remain explicitly unavailable.
- The snapshot covers the root project and resolved imported project content identities only. It does not prove complete environment, property, item, reference, or reconstruction parity and must not alone enable cache reuse.
- A project may import the same file more than once; occurrence order must be preserved rather than deduplicated.

