using System;

static Type M1<U>(I<U> t, U u) => typeof(U);

Console.WriteLine(M1(new Holder(), new B()).Name);  // 'U' is inferred as 'A' 
Console.WriteLine(M1<B>(new Holder(), new B()).Name); // But 'B' feels better

interface I<in T> { }
class A { }
class B : A { }
class Holder : I<A> { }
```

Observed:  For ```M1(new Holder(), new B())```  // 'U' is inferred as 'A' 

Expected: `B` feels like a better choice, especially that it is a valid one.

The behavior is likely according to the language spec. It adds type argument of ```I<A>``` as a lower bound, that automatically makes more specific bound ```B``` to lose. But in case of an 'in' type parameter, a more specific bound is probably a better choice.  Likely there is a similar issue for `out` type parameters.

